    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "837beecf-4239-4b3d-b683-1d611af3da2e",
      "key": "55878f9a-5e52-4232-b1cc-82f52ca5941e",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureTitle",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "Itinerary"
        },
        {
          "alias": "richTextContent",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Ch3\u003EDay 1\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe meet at our designated pick up points and transfer by private coach to Gatwick Airport for our direct scheduled Easyjet flight to the Isle of Man. On arrival we are met by our Blue Badge guide, and transfer by private coach to the 4* Empress Hotel at Douglas where we stay five nights on a half board basis.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 2\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOur Blue Badge guide meets us at our hotel this morning and accompanies us throughout the day. We firstly enjoy a scenic excursion from Ramsey to Laxey on the Manx Electric Railway, one of Britain\u2019s longest vintage narrow gauge railways. At Laxey, we explore the Great Laxey Wheel. Dating back to 1854 and known locally as \u2018Lady Isabella\u2019 after the wife of the island\u2019s governor at that time, it is the largest water wheel in the world and was designed by Robert Casement to pump water from the mineshafts. We then board the Snaefell Mountain Railway up to the summit of Snaefell.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 3\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOur full day with our Blue Badge guide begins at the Cregneash Village Folk Museum which provides a living, working illustration of the traditional customs, crafts and skills which characterised the Manx crofter\u2019s way of life. After free time for lunch at the old Manx capital of Castletown, we explore Castle Rushen. Guarding the entrance to Silverburn River and one of England\u0026#39;s best preserved medieval fortresses, the castle was taken by Robert the Bruce in 1313 who occupied the Isle of Man briefly whilst attempting to invade Ireland. The Castletown Steam Railway, opened in 1874 and still running with its original locomotives and the longest line of its type in Britain, takes us from Castletown to Douglas.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 4\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe firstly enjoy a ride on the Douglas Horse Trams with our Blue Badge guide. The trams were first introduced in 1876 to take advantage of the booming tourist trade in Victorian times and to this day still carry tourists and locals along the promenades. We then visit Peel, one of the world\u2019s smallest cathedral cities with just 3,800 residents. On arrival we see the remains of Peel Castle, occupying the important site of St Patrick\u2019s Isle at Peel. The castle\u2019s curtain wall encircles the ruins of many buildings which are a testimony to the site\u2019s religious and secular importance in Manx history. We then have some free time to explore the narrow lanes leading from the working harbour with their specialist and antique shops. This afternoon we visit Peel Cathedral, consecrated in 1980. Our day concludes with a visit to The House of Manannan heritage visitor centre, where, the Island\u2019s mythological sea god, Manannan, welcomes you to his fascinating Kingdom.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 5\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOur guided tour of the Gaiety Theatre this morning covers the auditorium, the backstage areas and the history of this iconic venue. This afternoon we visit the Milntown Estate House. During our guided tour we see elegant rooms and learn about the powerful Christian dynasty, who occupied Milntown for 500 years, and the vision of Sir Clive Edwards, who left the Estate in trust for the benefit of the Manx people.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 6\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe morning is at leisure in Douglas. With our Blue Badge guide this afternoon, and subject to availability, we enjoy a guided tour at Tynwald, the Parliament of the Isle of Man. Tynwald is of Norse origin and over 1,000 years old, and is thus the oldest parliament in the world. We then transfer to the airport for our direct scheduled Easyjet flight to Gatwick, and onwards private coach travel back to our original pick up points.\u003C/p\u003E\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureDescription",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": ""
        },
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureSummary",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003ENB:- Please note we reserve the right to alter the above itinerary if required for operational\nreasons. Given the historic nature of the places visited, a certain degree of walking will be involved, and this holiday may not be suitable for those with mobility problems.\u003C/p\u003E\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
